\section{Utility and production functions}{

Anything that is an R function of \code{x} and \code{y} is accepted by
\code{\link[=indifference_curve]{indifference_curve()}}, \code{\link[=optimal_bundle]{optimal_bundle()}}, \code{\link[=mrs]{mrs()}}, \code{\link[=expenditure]{expenditure()}},
\code{\link[=plot_consumer_choice]{plot_consumer_choice()}} and the rest; with no class it goes through the
numerical methods (bisection contours, a line search along the budget,
finite-difference MRS). To supply closed forms instead, give the function
a class and write the methods:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{stone_geary <- function(alpha, gamma_x, gamma_y) \{
  f <- function(x, y) (x - gamma_x)^alpha * (y - gamma_y)^(1 - alpha)
  structure(f, class = c("stone_geary", "function"),
            alpha = alpha, gamma_x = gamma_x, gamma_y = gamma_y, kind = "utility")
\}
optimal_bundle.stone_geary <- function(u, b, ...) \{ ... \}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Any method you do not write falls back to the numerical default, so a
partial set is fine. Follow the package's own pattern for the rest:
parameters as attributes, a \code{kind} attribute of \code{"utility"} or
\code{"production"} (the diagrams read it for their labels), and a \code{print()}
method.
}

\section{Demand and cost}{

\code{\link[=demand_fn]{demand_fn()}} already accepts any decreasing function of quantity, and
\code{\link[=production_cost]{production_cost()}} any production function, so most needs are met
without a new class. For closed forms, subclass the base class so the
generics' checks pass -- \code{class = c("isoelastic_demand", "demand")}, or
\code{c("cubic_cost", "cost")} -- and write methods for the generics you need:
\code{\link[=price_at]{price_at()}}, \code{\link[=quantity_at]{quantity_at()}}, \code{\link[=marginal_revenue]{marginal_revenue()}}, \code{\link[=consumer_surplus]{consumer_surplus()}}
for demand; \code{\link[=total_cost]{total_cost()}}, \code{\link[=marginal_cost]{marginal_cost()}}, \code{\link[=min_average_cost]{min_average_cost()}} for
cost. A demand object must carry \code{q_max}; a cost object must carry
\code{fixed}. Unimplemented generics fall through to the \code{general_demand} or
\code{production_cost} behaviour only if your object also carries what those
need (\code{p_of_q}, or \code{f}/\code{w}/\code{r}), so it is simpler to implement the full
set.
}

\section{Budgets}{

A \code{\link[=budget]{budget()}} is a plain list with \code{income}, \code{px}, \code{py}, \code{x_max}, \code{y_max}
and \code{slope}; there is nothing to extend, but a subclass
\code{c("my_budget", "budget")} carrying extra fields passes every check.
}

\section{Why the generics validate before dispatch}{

\code{\link[=price_at]{price_at()}} checks \code{inherits(d, "demand")} before calling
\code{UseMethod()}, and likewise for cost and budget objects. That rejects an
unrelated class with a clear message instead of a "no applicable method"
error deep inside a solver, at the price of requiring the subclassing
above. Utility functions are the exception: the check is only
\code{is.function()}, so any callable works.
}

\section{Two names that mean two things}{

\code{n} is a number of firms in \code{\link[=cournot]{cournot()}} and \code{\link[=perfect_competition]{perfect_competition()}} and a
number of consumers per type in \code{\link[=two_part_tariff]{two_part_tariff()}}; grid sizes are
\code{n_points}. \code{b} is a \code{\link[=budget]{budget()}} in the consumer functions and the
textbook coefficient in \code{\link[=quadratic_cost]{quadratic_cost()}}, \code{\link[=leontief]{leontief()}} and
\code{\link[=perfect_substitutes]{perfect_substitutes()}}; the coefficient keeps its letter because that is
what the formula is written with.
}

